---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/ui/SectionHead.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="About - Abject"
  description="About Abject and why I built it: an open-source, peer-to-peer desktop platform for AI objects that live on your own machine. Built by Maxim Khailo (mempko), the spiritual successor to Fire★. GPL-3.0, with no company, no investors, and no tracking."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="about">
    <div class="container">

      <header class="hero">
        <div class="hero-text">
          <p class="kicker"><span class="sigil" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>About</span></p>
          <h1 class="hero-title">I May Have Created Something Horrible</h1>
          <span class="hero-bar" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <p class="lede">
            Abject is an open-source desktop platform for AI objects that live on your own machine,
            find each other over peer-to-peer connections, and explain themselves in plain English.
            We call them Abjects. As in AI Object. As in abject horror. The name fits. It's licensed
            under GPL-3.0, it isn't a company, and it isn't selling you a subscription.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-mark" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 360 360" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
            <defs>
              <filter id="about-pupil-glow" x="-100%" y="-40%" width="300%" height="180%">
                <feGaussianBlur stdDeviation="6" result="b"/>
                <feMerge><feMergeNode in="b"/><feMergeNode in="SourceGraphic"/></feMerge>
              </filter>
            </defs>
            <!-- structure: rings and a slanted rule -->
            <circle cx="180" cy="180" r="168" fill="none" stroke="rgba(231,225,206,0.14)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <circle cx="180" cy="180" r="138" fill="none" stroke="rgba(231,225,206,0.28)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="2 7"/>
            <line x1="12" y1="300" x2="348" y2="228" stroke="rgba(231,225,206,0.28)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <!-- the red wedge crossing the eye -->
            <polygon points="0,236 360,122 360,168 0,282" fill="#d32f22" opacity="0.9"/>
            <!-- the eye -->
            <circle cx="180" cy="180" r="92" fill="#06070a" stroke="#d32f22" stroke-width="14"/>
            <ellipse class="pupil" cx="180" cy="180" rx="15" ry="58" fill="#5be5a0" filter="url(#about-pupil-glow)"/>
            <!-- small marks -->
            <rect x="300" y="36" width="18" height="18" fill="#d32f22"/>
            <rect x="326" y="36" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="#8d8778" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <polygon points="24,48 44,58 24,68" fill="#c9a45c"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section class="sec">
        <span class="sec-big" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
        <SectionHead num="01" title="Why I Built This" />
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Most people don't use computers to compute. They use them like an old media: they read
            text and look at images and videos. We've built a lot of closed systems with app stores
            so that people consume instead of create. That's possible because we engineers created
            information monopolies. People download an app because they don't have the knowledge to
            create their own. I don't think that's great for humanity. Now that LLMs can program
            competently in the small, regular people can create their own software that they control.
            Abject is an Abject-Oriented OS for people to be able to create software that's connected
            and personal to them, on their own machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            I also think AI agents are the wrong abstraction. They don't scale. Agent frameworks are
            hierarchical, with a single planner on top and tools beneath, and I'm deeply against
            hierarchies of any form. They work for short demos and break on the things you actually
            want done over weeks. The internet has no top, and it has kept running for more than fifty
            years.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is also a longer bet here. Software development over the last 60 years has been
            the equivalent of pyramid building: applications on frameworks on libraries on operating
            systems. Software grows until its parts no longer fit in an LLM's context window. I call
            this the Window Tax. Most of the industry is using LLMs to stack the pyramid faster. I would
            rather abandon the pyramid and start from a smaller foundation: objects sending messages,
            with the LLM handling the ambiguity that rigid interfaces never could.
          </p>
          <p>
            Objects talking to objects is what works. We have proof. It runs the world. The internet
            works this way, and so do the cells in your body. Smalltalk and Erlang were built on the
            same idea. Abject takes that pattern and adds one thing.
            Every object has a message handler called ask, so it can explain itself to any other
            object in plain English. The LLM is just a service an object asks when it needs to think.
            The architecture is older than the LLM.
          </p>
          <p>
            The result is a system you own. Your machine runs your agents. Your tokens come from your
            own account, or from a local model that costs nothing. Your goals live on your peers, not on
            someone else's server. If a provider changes its prices overnight, you point your abjects at
            a different model and keep going.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="sec">
        <span class="sec-big" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
        <SectionHead num="02" title="Who I Am" />
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            I'm <strong>Maxim Khailo</strong> (<a href="https://github.com/mempko" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mempko</a> on GitHub).
            I've been programming for 30 years, and I have always loved object-oriented systems like
            Smalltalk and Erlang.
          </p>
          <p>
            I came to the USA in the late 80s as a refugee from the former Soviet Union, from an area
            called Donbas. When I was in middle school my father got an old computer from a friend, and
            my love affair with technology and programming started. We were poor, so I learned
            programming by going to bookstores, reading the latest tech books, and trying to memorize
            them. Then I would go home and program. I read the ideas of Doug Engelbart and Alan Kay and
            fell in love with the idea that a computer can be a rocketship for the mind. But programming
            isn't easy, and most people don't know how to do it.
          </p>
          <p class="lineage">
            In 2014 I released <a href="https://firestr.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fire★</a>,
            the <em>Grass Computing</em> Platform. It let people build peer-to-peer apps with a built-in
            collaborative editor, and it was written in C++ and Lua and released under the GNU GPL. You'd
            create conversations and launch apps, and people could copy them and hack on them together,
            with no cloud in the middle. But people still needed to learn programming to use it to its
            fullest, and objects built by people who never met had no way to work out how to talk to each
            other. Abject is its spiritual successor.</p>
          <p class="whatif">
            What if objects could explain themselves? What if you didn't need to understand APIs to
            make software components work together? What if you didn't even need to know programming to
            use computers to their fullest? That's what the Abject project is about.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="sec">
        <SectionHead num="03" title="The Essays" />
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The short version of the argument is on <a href="/theory">the theory page</a>. These are
            the blog posts where I worked it out.
          </p>
          <ol class="ledger essays">
            <li><span class="ix" aria-hidden="true">01</span><strong class="t"><a href="https://blog.mempko.com/a-love-letter-to-object-orientation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A Love Letter to Object Orientation</a>.</strong> <span class="n">Alan Kay's big idea was messaging, not classes. Why an object explaining itself is compression, not abstraction, and why the objects are the expendable part.</span></li>
            <li><span class="ix" aria-hidden="true">02</span><strong class="t"><a href="https://blog.mempko.com/an-abject-horror/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">An Abject Horror</a>.</strong> <span class="n">The announcement. Why AI agents are the wrong abstraction, and what a self-aware object runtime is.</span></li>
            <li><span class="ix" aria-hidden="true">03</span><strong class="t"><a href="https://blog.mempko.com/entering-the-architecture-age/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Entering the Architecture Age</a>.</strong> <span class="n">The software pyramid, the Window Tax, and why the big idea is messaging.</span></li>
            <li><span class="ix" aria-hidden="true">04</span><strong class="t"><a href="https://blog.mempko.com/let-the-information-monopolies-crumble/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Let the Information Monopolies Crumble!</a></strong> <span class="n">Why people should use computers to compute, and why the information monopolies should crumble.</span></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="sec">
        <SectionHead num="04" title="What Abject Isn't" />
        <ul class="mosaic nots">
          <li class="not-startup solid-red"><strong>Not a startup.</strong> There are no investors, no board, and no exit strategy. Nobody has a plan to monetize you.</li>
          <li class="not-saas outline"><strong>Not a SaaS.</strong> Your data, your tokens, and your machine stay yours. The signaling server is open source, so you can run your own.</li>
          <li class="not-telemetry paper"><strong>Not telemetry-funded.</strong> Nothing phones home. The only connections it makes are to the model provider you chose, to the signaling server that introduces peers (open source, run your own), and to whatever an abject is asked to fetch, within the domain rules you set.</li>
          <li class="not-closed"><strong>Not closed.</strong> It's GPL-3.0-or-later. Fork it, change it, ship it. Just keep it free.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="sec">
        <SectionHead num="05" title="How To Reach Me" />
        <ul class="contact">
          <li><strong>Email:</strong> <span><a href="mailto:max@mempko.com">max@mempko.com</a></span></li>
          <li><strong>GitHub:</strong> <span><a href="https://github.com/mempko/abject" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mempko/abject</a> (issues, PRs welcome)</span></li>
          <li><strong>Reddit:</strong> <span><a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/AbjectWorld/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">r/AbjectWorld</a></span></li>
          <li><strong>Discord:</strong> <span><a href="https://discord.gg/B75AeYUYcF" target="_blank" rel="noopener">join here</a></span></li>
        </ul>
      </section>

    </div>

    <section class="field-red cut-top coda-field" aria-label="Coda">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="poster-quote coda">
          Abject is a long-term project, and it will stay free. Try it, break it, build something horrible with it. <span class="coda-free">Join the horror.</span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  /* ── Page frame ── */
  .about {
    padding-top: calc(69px + clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5.5rem));
  }

  /* ── Hero: the poster column plus the eye, offset right ── */
  .hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.5fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4.5rem);
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 2rem);
  }

  .hero-title {
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 5.4vw, 4.5rem);
    line-height: 0.98;
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }

  .hero-bar {
    display: block;
    width: 180px;
    height: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 1.6rem;
    background: var(--red);
    clip-path: polygon(0 0, 100% 0, calc(100% - 10px) 100%, 0 100%);
  }

  .hero .lede {
    font-size: clamp(1.06rem, 1.45vw, 1.2rem);
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .hero-mark { justify-self: end; width: 100%; max-width: 380px; }
  .hero-mark svg { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }
  .pupil { animation: breathe 7s ease-in-out infinite; }

  /* ── Sections ── */
  .sec {
    position: relative;
    padding: clamp(3.5rem, 7vw, 5.5rem) 0 0;
  }

  .sec :global(.section-head) { margin-bottom: 2rem; }

  /* Big outlined numeral in the right margin of the two prose sections. */
  .sec-big {
    position: absolute;
    right: 0;
    top: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(8rem, 15vw, 14rem);
    line-height: 0.8;
    color: transparent;
    -webkit-text-stroke: 1.5px var(--rule-mid);
    pointer-events: none;
    user-select: none;
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: 760px;
    position: relative;
  }

  .prose p {
    color: var(--bone-2);
    font-size: 1.06rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-bottom: 1.15rem;
  }

  .prose p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  /* Fire★: a note hung on a brass rule. */
    .prose .whatif {
    margin-top: 1.8rem;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--bone);
  }

  .lineage {
    margin-top: 1.6rem;
    padding: 0.2rem 0 0.2rem 1.4rem;
    border-left: 6px solid var(--brass);
  }

  /* ── 03: the essays as a ledger ── */
  .essays {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 1.8rem 0 0;
  }

  .essays .t { line-height: 1.3; }

  .essays .t a {
    color: var(--bone-heading);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: rgba(91, 229, 160, 0.45);
    text-decoration-thickness: 1.5px;
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }

  .essays .t a:hover { color: var(--living); text-decoration-color: var(--red); }
  .essays .n { line-height: 1.65; }

  /* ── 04: what this isn't, as a mosaic ── */
  .nots {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    grid-auto-rows: auto;
    grid-template-areas:
      'startup saas tele tele'
      'closed closed tele tele';
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--bone-2);
  }

  .not-startup { grid-area: startup; }
  .not-saas { grid-area: saas; }
  .not-telemetry { grid-area: tele; }
  .not-closed { grid-area: closed; }

  .nots .solid-red { color: rgba(246, 239, 219, 0.9); }
  .nots .paper { color: #3f392f; }

  .nots strong {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    line-height: 1.1;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bone-heading);
  }

  .nots .solid-red strong { color: var(--on-red); }
  .nots .paper strong { color: #16130f; font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 2.8vw, 2.3rem); }
  .nots .not-closed strong { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2.4vw, 2rem); }
  .nots .paper { font-size: 1.08rem; }

  /* A struck square: the mark for a thing this is not. */
  .nots strong::before {
    content: '';
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 16px;
    height: 16px;
    border: 2px solid var(--red);
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, transparent 44%, var(--red) 44%, var(--red) 56%, transparent 56%);
  }

  .nots .solid-red strong::before {
    border-color: var(--on-red);
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, transparent 44%, var(--on-red) 44%, var(--on-red) 56%, transparent 56%);
  }

  /* ── 05: contact columns between heavy rules ── */
  .contact {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    border-top: 3px solid var(--bone-heading);
    border-bottom: var(--rule-w) solid var(--rule-strong);
  }

  .contact li {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    padding: 1.3rem 1.3rem 1.5rem 0;
    color: var(--bone-3);
  }

  .contact li + li { padding-left: 1.3rem; border-left: 1px solid var(--rule-mid); }

  .contact strong {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--red);
  }

  .contact a {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 1.8vw, 1.5rem);
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--bone-heading);
    word-break: break-word;
  }

  .contact a:hover { color: var(--living); }

  /* ── Coda: the closing line on the red field ── */
  .coda-field {
    margin-top: clamp(4rem, 8vw, 6rem);
  }

  .coda {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4.4vw, 3.4rem);
    max-width: 1060px;
    color: var(--on-red);
  }

  .coda-free { color: #16130f; }

  /* ── Responsive ── */
  @media (max-width: 1080px) {
    .sec-big { display: none; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .hero { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .hero-mark { justify-self: start; max-width: 220px; }
    .nots { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); grid-template-areas: 'startup saas' 'tele tele' 'closed closed'; }
    .contact { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .contact li + li:nth-child(3) { padding-left: 0; border-left: none; }
    .contact li:nth-child(n + 3) { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-mid); }
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .nots { grid-template-columns: 1fr; grid-template-areas: 'startup' 'saas' 'tele' 'closed'; }
    .contact { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .contact li, .contact li + li { padding-left: 0; border-left: none; }
    .contact li + li { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-mid); }
  }
</style>
